// Multi-module imports + prelude guard, in Bit (port of the deleted harness
// `imports`, #1153).
//
// Every direct subdirectory of the corpus is a root module (its `main`); it may
// import project-relative modules (`./util`) and standard-library modules
// (`std/core`), and it sees the auto-imported prelude. This harness builds each
// one with the self-hosted `bit` and diffs the program's stdout against the
// project's checked-in `expected` — so cross-module lowering and the prelude can
// never silently regress.
//
// Configuration is by ENVIRONMENT, not argv: `bit run <file>` forwards no
// arguments to the program it launches (compiler/build.bit `runCmd`), so an
// argv-shaped config would read empty and this harness would run over nothing.
// Every variable is validated and a bad one is a loud failure, never a skip:
//
//   BIT_IMPORTS_DIR     absolute path to the corpus (_tests_/imports)   required
//   BIT_IMPORTS_BIT     absolute path to the `bit` under test         required
//   BIT_STDLIB          absolute path to stdlib/                      required
//   BIT_LIBBITRT        absolute path to the target's libbitrt.a      required
//   BIT_IMPORTS_FILTER  run one named project (must match exactly)    optional
//   BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S  per-child wall clock, default 300             optional
//                       (#3593: a case that misses this once, under
//                       contention, gets one serialized retry before it is
//                       graded as failed — see the design note below and
//                       `grading.bit`'s `retryTimedOut`)
//
// WHY A SHELL WRAPPER. `osRunBounded` (ABI.md §19) is the only bounded child
// wait Bit has, and it takes a path and nothing else — no argv, no env, no
// stdout capture. So each case gets two generated `/bin/sh` scripts, and the
// shell supplies the three missing pieces, plus (below) its own parallelism —
// see `writeScripts` and `driverScript`.
//
// `bit_rt_os_run_bounded` IS STILL NOT RE-ENTRANT, and nothing here changes
// that (#2508 does not touch it — that is `runtime/root/**`, a mandatory-full-
// suite change, and out of scope). Its child argv, wait-status cell and
// timespec scratch are module-level and unlocked (`osArgvBuf`, `osStatusBuf`,
// `osChildEnvp`, `osIdxBuf`, `osClockBuf` in
// `runtime/root/{linux,darwin}/os.bit`), sound only under the single-worker
// contract — "a `run` fully completes (fork through wait) before any other
// `run` can begin". A `spawn`ed pool of green threads each calling it would
// race on that shared state.
//
// So the concurrency lives in the SHELL instead, which this harness already
// delegated env/argv/stdout capture to. This file makes one `osRunBounded`
// call per WAVE (compile, then run) rather than one for the whole corpus —
// the driver, not Bit green threads, is what fans out within each wave:
// `find | xargs -P` does in the shell exactly what a re-entrant
// `osRunBounded` would otherwise have had to do inside the runtime.
//
// TWO WAVES, BARRIERED, NOT ONE. `fork` duplicates the fd table, so a child
// spawned while another process still holds a write fd open to some binary
// inherits it, and a later `execve` of that inode fails with ETXTBSY — a
// real, already-paid-for bug (74811a3). A serial harness has no such window;
// a parallel one does, UNLESS every compile's write fd is closed before any
// run forks. So `runDriver` below runs the compile wave's `osRunBounded`
// call to completion — its child reaped whether by a clean exit or by the
// timeout/signal path's own `waitpid` (`osForkExecWaitBounded`,
// runtime/root/*/os.bit) — before the run wave's call even starts. Two
// sequential calls are the whole barrier now, same as two sequential
// `find | xargs` lines inside one script used to be. Collapsing either wave
// into the other would reopen the window 74811a3 closed.
//
// PER-CASE DEADLINES MOVE INTO THE GENERATED SCRIPTS THEMSELVES: each backs
// its work with a `sleep $T; kill -9` watchdog and reports through a `.rc`
// file (`decodeRc`/`readRcFile`), not an `osRunBounded` return value.
//
// THE OUTER BOUND IS PER WAVE, NOT PER CORPUS (#3586). A single call over
// both waves and every case used to be "generous rather than tight" by
// design — but `osRunBounded` clamps any request over `osBoundedMaxMs`
// (`runtime/root/*/os.bit`) down to that value regardless of the request, so
// the old "2x every case at once" claim was silently never honoured past a few
// dozen cases at the default 300s budget: the harness computed ~24h, the
// runtime clamped that down to `osBoundedMaxMs`, and under real fleet load
// that ceiling was not always enough — with the same call the ONLY thing
// between "driver hung" and "every case finished", the whole corpus died with
// no per-case attribution.
// `runDriver` now bounds each wave separately (`waveTimeoutMs`, sized off
// REAL `xargs -P` parallelism, not a serial worst case), and `main` grades
// every `.rc` file regardless of how either call returned, so an overrun
// kills only the cases still in flight.
//
// A PER-CASE WATCHDOG FIRING IS NOT NECESSARILY A HANG (#3593). The 300s
// per-case budget above has no contention allowance of its own, unlike this
// wave-level bound — under real fleet load (this box runs ~20 agents; a
// `boxlock` slot bounds concurrent BUILDS, not everything else running) a
// correct case can be starved past its own watchdog's deadline with no bug
// in the case at all. `grading.bit`'s `retryTimedOut` gives such a case one
// retry, run ALONE rather than inside this wave's `xargs -P n` fan-out, so a
// starved-not-hung case gets a fair shot without raising the budget (which
// would only move the false-failure threshold, not remove it) — see that
// file's header for why a genuine hang is still caught, unchanged.
//
// SPLIT ACROSS TWO FILES, SAME MODULE (#3593): this directory is the module
// (`compiler/project.bit`'s "a module is a DIRECTORY" contract), so grading
// and the retry live in the sibling `grading.bit` with no import needed —
// purely to stay under the repo's 800-line file ceiling. #3163 made
// cross-file inlining order-independent, so the split names by readability,
// not load order (see `bitlang-ws/CLAUDE.md`'s splitting section).
//
// This file deliberately uses only universe builtins (ABI boundary primitives)
// and no `std/*` import, so it runs under a bare `bit run` with no BIT_STDLIB
// needed to compile the harness itself. The one import below is a relative,
// zero-import leaf (`tools/killtree/`, #3238) that adds no transitive `std/*`
// dependency — verified with a real `bit run` under an unset BIT_STDLIB, not
// just by reading its own imports (see that module's header for why it lives
// in neither `tools/build/` nor `_tests_/bit/`).
import { killTreeFnDef, pgrepPreflightScript } from "../../../tools/killtree"

// The corpus size this gate refuses to run below. The deleted harness had only an
// UPPER bound (`scanned < max_programs`) and an explicit `if (cases.len == 0)
// return` — an empty corpus was a green pass. A floor is the whole difference
// between a gate and a decoration: raise it when projects are added.
const caseFloor: int = 99

// Upper bound on directory entries examined (Power of 10 rule 2).
const maxEntries: int = 4096

const defaultTimeoutS: int = 300

// Per-wave slack on top of the round-count estimate (`waveTimeoutMs` below)
// — covers `find`/`xargs`/process-startup overhead the round math itself
// does not model. Shared by both waves; each gets this once, not doubled.
const outerSlackMs: int = 60000

// 0o755 — the scripts must be executable by the `fork`+`execve` in `xargs`
// (phase B) and, for the driver itself, in `osRunBounded`.
const scriptMode: int = 493

class Cfg {
  corpus: string,
  bitExe: string,
  stdlib: string,
  libbitrt: string,
  filter: string,
  timeoutMs: int,
}

class Case {
  name: string,
  dir: string,
  expected: string,
  compileSh: string,
  runSh: string,
  bin: string,
  log: string,
  out: string,
  err: string,
  // Written by `compileSh`/`runSh` themselves (`echo "$rc" > …`), since phase
  // B no longer makes a per-case `osRunBounded` call whose return value could
  // carry this instead.
  compileRc: string,
  runRc: string,
}

class Text {
  ok: bool,
  data: string,
}

// --- small utilities (no std/*, no prelude) --------------------------------

fn readWhole(path: string): Text {
  let fd = fsOpen(path, false)
  if (fd < 0) {
    return Text{ ok: false, data: "" }
  }
  let s = fsReadAll(fd)
  fsClose(fd)
  return Text{ ok: true, data: s }
}

fn writeWhole(path: string, content: string): bool {
  let fd = fsOpen(path, true)
  if (fd < 0) {
    return false
  }
  let n = fsWrite(fd, content)
  fsClose(fd)
  return n >= 0
}

// Byte-wise `a < b`, so a listing is reproducible across filesystems.
fn lessStr(a: string, b: string): bool {
  let n = len(a)
  if (len(b) < n) {
    n = len(b)
  }
  let i = 0
  while (i < n) {
    if (a[i] != b[i]) {
      return a[i] < b[i]
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return len(a) < len(b)
}

fn sortStrings(xs: []string): []string {
  let i = 1
  while (i < len(xs)) {
    let j = i
    while (j > 0 && lessStr(xs[j], xs[j - 1])) {
      let t = xs[j - 1]
      xs[j - 1] = xs[j]
      xs[j] = t
      j = j - 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return xs
}

// `fsListDir` returns entries NUL-separated: NUL is the one byte a POSIX
// filename cannot contain.
fn splitNul(raw: string): []string {
  let out = []string(0)
  let start = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(raw) && len(out) < maxEntries) {
    if (raw[i] == 0) {
      if (i > start) {
        out = append(out, raw[start:i])
      }
      start = i + 1
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out
}

// Digits only, and short enough that `v * 10` cannot overflow i64.
const maxIntDigits: int = 9

fn parseNonNegInt(s: string): int {
  if (len(s) == 0 || len(s) > maxIntDigits) {
    return -1
  }
  let v = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(s)) {
    let c = s[i]
    if (c < 48 || c > 57) {
      return -1
    }
    v = v * 10 + int(c) - 48
    i = i + 1
  }
  return v
}

// A path is interpolated into a double-quoted shell word, so anything the shell
// would still expand or terminate there must be rejected outright rather than
// quoted around. Rejecting is safe: every real path in this repo is plain.
fn shSafe(s: string): bool {
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(s)) {
    let c = s[i]
    if (c == 34 || c == 36 || c == 96 || c == 92 || c == 10 || c == 13 || c == 0) {
      return false
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return true
}

fn fatal(msg: string) {
  eprint("imports: FAIL ${msg}\n")
  osExit(1)
}

// --- configuration ---------------------------------------------------------

// Every path must be ABSOLUTE. A relative corpus path resolved against whatever
// CWD the harness happens to inherit is the single most likely way this gate
// goes silently vacuous — `fsListDir` on a missing directory yields nothing,
// which without this check reads as "0 failed".
fn requireDir(name: string): string {
  let v = osEnv(name)
  if (len(v) == 0) {
    fatal("${name} is unset; it must be an absolute directory path")
  }
  if (v[0] != 47) {
    fatal("${name}='${v}' is not absolute")
  }
  if (!fsIsDir(v)) {
    fatal("${name}='${v}' is not a directory")
  }
  if (!shSafe(v)) {
    fatal("${name}='${v}' contains a character this harness will not put in a shell word")
  }
  return v
}

fn requireFile(name: string): string {
  let v = osEnv(name)
  if (len(v) == 0) {
    fatal("${name} is unset; it must be an absolute file path")
  }
  if (v[0] != 47) {
    fatal("${name}='${v}' is not absolute")
  }
  if (!fsExists(v) || fsIsDir(v)) {
    fatal("${name}='${v}' is not a file")
  }
  if (!shSafe(v)) {
    fatal("${name}='${v}' contains a character this harness will not put in a shell word")
  }
  return v
}

fn loadConfig(): Cfg {
  let corpus = requireDir("BIT_IMPORTS_DIR")
  let bitExe = requireFile("BIT_IMPORTS_BIT")
  let stdlib = requireDir("BIT_STDLIB")
  let libbitrt = requireFile("BIT_LIBBITRT")

  let secs = defaultTimeoutS
  let raw = osEnv("BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S")
  if (len(raw) != 0) {
    let parsed = parseNonNegInt(raw)
    if (parsed <= 0) {
      // The deleted harness reads 0 as "block forever". There is no unbounded form
      // of `osRunBounded`, and a 0 ms budget would kill every child instantly,
      // so an unusable value is refused instead of silently reinterpreted.
      fatal("BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S='${raw}' is not a positive integer")
    }
    secs = parsed
  }

  let filter = osEnv("BIT_IMPORTS_FILTER")
  if (len(filter) != 0 && !shSafe(filter)) {
    fatal("BIT_IMPORTS_FILTER='${filter}' is not a plain name")
  }

  return Cfg{
    corpus: corpus, bitExe: bitExe, stdlib: stdlib, libbitrt: libbitrt,
    filter: filter, timeoutMs: secs * 1000,
  }
}

// --- discovery -------------------------------------------------------------

// Every immediate subdirectory of cfg.corpus, sorted.
fn listProjectDirs(cfg: Cfg): []string {
  let names = sortStrings(splitNul(fsListDir(cfg.corpus)))
  let dirs = []string(0)
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(names)) {
    let n = names[i]
    if (fsIsDir("${cfg.corpus}/${n}")) {
      dirs = append(dirs, n)
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  return dirs
}

// Narrows `dirs` to cfg.filter when set (BIT_IMPORTS_FILTER), else asserts
// the corpus is at least caseFloor projects wide.
fn filterProjectDirs(cfg: Cfg, dirs: []string): []string {
  if (len(cfg.filter) != 0) {
    let kept = []string(0)
    let j = 0
    while (j < len(dirs)) {
      if (dirs[j] == cfg.filter) {
        kept = append(kept, dirs[j])
      }
      j = j + 1
    }
    if (len(kept) == 0) {
      fatal("BIT_IMPORTS_FILTER='${cfg.filter}' matched no project under ${cfg.corpus}")
    }
    return kept
  }
  if (len(dirs) < caseFloor) {
    fatal("discovered ${len(dirs)} project(s) under ${cfg.corpus}, below the floor of ${caseFloor} — the corpus is wrong or has shrunk")
  }
  return dirs
}

// Validates project `name` under cfg.corpus and builds its Case record.
// Fail, never skip. A missing `main.bit` or a missing/empty `expected` is
// how 96 cases turn into 0 with no change in the output.
fn buildCase(cfg: Cfg, scratch: string, name: string): Case {
  if (!shSafe(name)) {
    fatal("project name '${name}' is not a plain name")
  }
  let dir = "${cfg.corpus}/${name}"
  if (!fsExists("${dir}/main.bit")) {
    fatal("project '${name}' has no main.bit")
  }
  let exp = readWhole("${dir}/expected")
  if (!exp.ok) {
    fatal("project '${name}' has no readable 'expected' file")
  }
  if (len(exp.data) == 0) {
    fatal("project '${name}' has an EMPTY 'expected' file; an empty comparison proves nothing")
  }
  let base = "${scratch}/${name}"
  return Case{
    name: name, dir: dir, expected: exp.data,
    compileSh: "${base}.compile.sh", runSh: "${base}.run.sh", bin: "${base}.bin",
    log: "${base}.log", out: "${base}.out", err: "${base}.err",
    compileRc: "${base}.compile.rc", runRc: "${base}.run.rc",
  }
}

fn discover(cfg: Cfg, scratch: string): []Case {
  let dirs = filterProjectDirs(cfg, listProjectDirs(cfg))
  let cases = []Case(0)
  let k = 0
  while (k < len(dirs)) {
    cases = append(cases, buildCase(cfg, scratch, dirs[k]))
    k = k + 1
  }
  return cases
}

// --- phase A: write every script, fork nothing -----------------------------

// Builds the shell text a case's watchdog polls against. `wdPath` is the
// `bit-gate-watchdog` symlink `driverScript` creates once, in `scratch`,
// before either fan-out wave starts (read, never written, by every case's
// poll). `budgetS` is the wall-clock deadline in seconds baked into the
// script TEXT at generation time — not read at run time, so a script always
// polls against whatever budget it was generated with. `retryTimedOut`
// (`grading.bit`) calls this a second time with a LARGER `budgetS` to
// regenerate a case's scripts for its one serialized retry: rerunning the
// exact bytes phase A wrote would poll the identical threshold and die
// identically under the same fleet contention that killed the first attempt
// (#3665).
//
// Each script backgrounds its real work (`cmd &`) and captures that exact
// child's pid in `$p`, then races it against a watchdog running as a
// second background job (`$w`) — the per-job deadline a shared wave now
// needs, since a whole wave of cases goes through one outer
// `osRunBounded` call rather than one each. `wait "$p"` reports that
// specific child's status (128+9=137 on our own SIGKILL, since nothing
// else in this closed system sends it, which is what `decodeRc` below
// reads back out of the `.rc` file). `kill "$w"` reaps the watchdog once
// the real work is done so it does not outlive the script. No `exec`:
// the backgrounded command is already the direct child (no wrapping
// shell to hand status through), and control has to return to THIS
// script afterward to write the `.rc` file.
//
// `$w`'s own stdio is detached (`</dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1`) rather than
// left to inherit this script's — #2508's fanout runs one of these per
// case, and on the success path the explicit `wait "$w"` right after
// `kill "$w"` reaps it before this script exits, so bash never gets a
// chance to report it later as an unsolicited "Terminated: 15" job (that
// notice is what #2514 saw flooding a passing `test-imports-bit`'s own
// output — a race between exit and bash's async job-status check, not
// conditional on job-control/monitor mode).
//
// #2563: `$w` used to be one `sleep $T; kill -9`. `kill "$w"` on the
// success path reaches only that subshell, not the `sleep` it forked (sh
// cannot exec-replace into `sleep` here because a `kill -9` follows it),
// so a SIGTERM'd subshell does not propagate to that child — `sleep`
// reparented to pid 1 and ran out its own clock, up to `$T` later (224
// measured after one `test-imports-bit` run, #2514's own count). `$w` now
// POLLS instead of sleeping once: `kill -0 "$p"` every second, so on the
// success path it notices `$p` is gone and exits within ~1s — normally
// before `kill "$w"` below even runs — and even a lost race leaves only a
// `sleep 1` behind, which finishes on its own within that same ~1s rather
// than `$T`.
//
// #3231: the poll used to be `( exec -a bit-gate-watchdog sleep 1 )`.
// `exec -a` (rename argv[0] before exec) is a bash/ksh extension; dash —
// Debian's `/bin/sh`, and therefore this project's own x64 gate image —
// rejects `-a` outright, so the subshell errored out INSTANTLY instead of
// sleeping, `n` raced through the whole timeout budget in a fraction of a
// real second, and every compile/run job here was killed almost
// immediately on any dash host — the exact bug #3227 had already fixed
// for `tools/build/gatesexec.bit`'s batch watchdog, independently duplicated
// here. `driverScript` below now symlinks a `bit-gate-watchdog` name onto
// the real `sleep` binary ONCE, in `scratch`, before either fan-out wave
// starts (`wdPath`); every case's poll execs that shared symlink
// directly, which gives the poll's `sleep` process the same argv[0]
// rename through a plain, extension-free exec (POSIX: argv[0] is
// whatever name you invoke) with no shell-detection branch, and a
// `|| sleep 1` fallback keeps the poll correct even if the symlink is
// somehow missing.
//
// Was kept as a SECOND COPY of #3227's mechanism through #3231/#3237, for
// reasons that #3238 now supersedes: `tools/build/gates.bit` imports
// `std/os`, and a Bit import is per-MODULE not per-file, so importing
// anything FROM `gates.bit` would have dragged that whole `std/os`
// dependency into this file — contradicting its own header comment ("no
// `std/*` import, so it runs under a bare `bit run` with no BIT_STDLIB
// needed to compile the harness itself"). #3238 resolved that by adding
// a THIRD, zero-import leaf module (`tools/killtree/`, imported above)
// holding just the shared shell-text producers, importable by both sides
// without either depending on the other. `killTreeFnDef()` is called
// here; each standalone per-case script still gets its own copy of the
// shell TEXT baked in at generation time (there is no common parent
// script here to hold one shared runtime definition, unlike
// `tools/build/gatesexec.bit`'s batch script) — sharing the producer
// function does not require sharing the emission granularity.
//
// #3066: `kill -9 "$p"` on the deadline branch only killed `$p` itself.
// `$p` here is `bit build`/the compiled test binary — either can fork
// its own children, which then ran on orphaned past this case's own
// deadline. `killtree` walks `$p`'s descendants by PPID (`pgrep -P`,
// Power-of-10-bounded to 20 levels), over THREE full walks before
// killing anything (a late spawn is invisible to one pass and its own
// ancestor may already be dead by the time a second pass looks — see
// `killTreeFnDef`'s own comment in `tools/killtree/killtree.bit`,
// verified there against `test-imports-bit`'s own continuously-
// respawning `xargs -P` wave), then kills every discovered PID
// individually — the same fix and the same reasoning as
// `gateBatchUnit()` in `tools/build/gatesexec.bit` (read that comment for
// why a process GROUP was tried and rejected).
fn watchdogScript(wdPath: string, budgetS: int): string {
  return killTreeFnDef() +
    "n=0; while kill -0 \"$p\" 2>/dev/null; do if [ \"$n\" -ge ${budgetS} ]; then killtree \"$p\"; break; fi; \"${wdPath}\" 1 || sleep 1; n=$((n + 1)); done"
}

fn compileScriptText(cfg: Cfg, c: Case, watchdog: string): string {
  return "#!/bin/sh\nexport BIT_STDLIB=\"${cfg.stdlib}\"\nexport BIT_LIBBITRT=\"${cfg.libbitrt}\"\n\"${cfg.bitExe}\" build \"${c.dir}\" -o \"${c.bin}\" >\"${c.log}\" 2>&1 &\np=$!\n( ${watchdog} ) </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &\nw=$!\nwait \"$p\"\nrc=$?\nkill \"$w\" 2>/dev/null\nwait \"$w\" 2>/dev/null\necho \"$rc\" > \"${c.compileRc}\"\nexit \"$rc\"\n"
}

// BIT_SELF_EXE is a real coupling, not decoration: _tests_/imports/pmadd_e2e
// execs the compiler that built it (`bit add`) and panics without it. The
// shell also hands the child the full inherited environment, which that
// case's git/HOME/PATH usage needs.
fn runScriptText(cfg: Cfg, c: Case, watchdog: string): string {
  return "#!/bin/sh\nexport BIT_SELF_EXE=\"${cfg.bitExe}\"\n\"${c.bin}\" >\"${c.out}\" 2>\"${c.err}\" &\np=$!\n( ${watchdog} ) </dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1 &\nw=$!\nwait \"$p\"\nrc=$?\nkill \"$w\" 2>/dev/null\nwait \"$w\" 2>/dev/null\necho \"$rc\" > \"${c.runRc}\"\nexit \"$rc\"\n"
}

fn writeScripts(cfg: Cfg, cases: []Case, scratch: string) {
  let wdPath = "${scratch}/bit-gate-watchdog"
  let watchdog = watchdogScript(wdPath, cfg.timeoutMs / 1000)
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(cases)) {
    let c = cases[i]
    if (!writeWhole(c.compileSh, compileScriptText(cfg, c, watchdog)) ||
      fsChmod(c.compileSh, scriptMode) != 0) {
      fatal("cannot write compile script for '${c.name}' at ${c.compileSh}")
    }
    if (!writeWhole(c.runSh, runScriptText(cfg, c, watchdog)) ||
      fsChmod(c.runSh, scriptMode) != 0) {
      fatal("cannot write run script for '${c.name}' at ${c.runSh}")
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
}

// --- phase B: one shell driver PER WAVE, barriered by two sequential calls -

// The whole fan-out for one wave. `-print0`/`-0` so a case name can never be
// split by `xargs`'s default whitespace/newline delimiting (case names are
// validated plain by `shSafe`, but that does not rule out a space).
// `xargs -P "$N" -n1 sh` (#3789, mirroring gatefanout.bit's #3778): the
// script PATH becomes sh's own script argument — data sh reads and
// interprets — rather than a program the kernel execve()s. That distinction
// is load-bearing on macOS: a freshly-created executable's FIRST direct
// execve() (the old `sh -c 'exec "$0"'` form, which execs the job's own
// inode via its `#!/bin/sh` line) pays a one-time Gatekeeper/AMFI
// validation, ~250-450ms, per never-before-run file, serialized system-wide
// rather than hidden by `-P`. `sh <path>` never execve()s the case script at
// all, so the tax never fires. This works identically with `-P` (parallel)
// on both GNU and BSD (macOS) `xargs`, unlike `-I`. The
// exit code returned is `xargs`'s own for THIS wave: nonzero means at least
// one case's script itself exited nonzero, which `gradeCase` is what names
// — `main` still reads it back and, if grading found NO failing case at all
// despite `xargs` reporting one, treats that as its own fatal error.
//
// #3236: `killtree()`'s `pgrep -P` blind spot ("not installed" vs "no
// descendants" both read as empty) is fixed once, in `tools/killtree/`
// (imported above), and shared by `gatesexec.bit`'s own copy. Its preflight
// (`pgrepPreflightScript`) runs once per wave here — twice total, harmless,
// read-only — rather than once per case, since each wave's own script is a
// common parent that runs once before its fan-out forks. Warn, not refuse:
// a case only calls `killtree` after blowing `BIT_TEST_TIMEOUT_S`, and
// whatever it fails to reap without `pgrep` is still bounded by that wave's
// own `osRunBounded` deadline (`waveTimeoutMs`, `runDriver` below).
//
// The `bit-gate-watchdog` symlink is created fresh at the top of each wave's
// script, before that wave's `find` runs — idempotent (`ln -sf`), so twice
// (once per wave) is harmless; every case's poll (`wdPath` in
// `writeScripts`) relies on it existing by the time its own script forks.
fn waveDriverScript(scratch: string, pattern: string, boundName: string): string {
  return "#!/bin/sh\n" +
    pgrepPreflightScript("case", boundName) +
    "ln -sf \"$(command -v sleep)\" \"${scratch}/bit-gate-watchdog\" 2>/dev/null\n" +
    "N=$(getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN 2>/dev/null || sysctl -n hw.ncpu 2>/dev/null || nproc 2>/dev/null || echo 4)\n" +
    "find \"${scratch}\" -maxdepth 1 -name '${pattern}' -print0 | xargs -0 -P \"$N\" -n1 sh\n" +
    "exit $?\n"
}

// --- cleanup + report ------------------------------------------------------

fn cleanup(scratch: string, cases: []Case, compileSh: string, runSh: string) {
  let i = 0
  while (i < len(cases)) {
    let c = cases[i]
    fsRemove(c.compileSh)
    fsRemove(c.runSh)
    fsRemove(c.compileRc)
    fsRemove(c.runRc)
    fsRemove(c.bin)
    fsRemove(c.log)
    fsRemove(c.out)
    fsRemove(c.err)
    i = i + 1
  }
  fsRemove(compileSh)
  fsRemove(runSh)
  // `fsRemove` on a directory is `rmdir` (empty dirs only, ABI.md §14) — the
  // `bit-gate-watchdog` symlink `waveDriverScript` creates in `scratch` must
  // be unlinked explicitly first, or every run leaks one symlink into
  // `$TMPDIR` forever (`fsRemove(scratch)` below would keep silently failing
  // on it).
  fsRemove("${scratch}/bit-gate-watchdog")
  fsRemove(scratch)
}

fn scratchRoot(): string {
  let base = osEnv("TMPDIR")
  if (len(base) == 0) {
    base = "/tmp"
  }
  if (base[len(base) - 1] == 47) {
    base = base[0:len(base) - 1]
  }
  return "${base}/bit-importsrun-${timeUnixNs()}"
}

class DriverRun {
  compileSh: string,
  runSh: string,
  compileResult: int,
  runResult: int,
}

fn detectParallelism(scratch: string): int {
  // Real parallelism, not a guess: same `getconf`/`sysctl`/`nproc` chain
  // `waveDriverScript` hands `xargs -P`, so `waveTimeoutMs` sizes each wave's
  // bound off what `xargs` will actually use. Bounded at 10s — this call
  // does not scale with caseCount.
  let sh = "${scratch}/nproc.sh"
  let out = "${scratch}/nproc.txt"
  let script = "#!/bin/sh\n{ getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN 2>/dev/null || sysctl -n hw.ncpu 2>/dev/null || nproc 2>/dev/null || echo 4; } > \"${out}\"\n"
  if (!writeWhole(sh, script) || fsChmod(sh, scriptMode) != 0) {
    fatal("cannot write nproc probe script at ${sh}")
  }
  osRunBounded(sh, 10000)
  let raw = readWhole(out).data
  if (len(raw) != 0 && raw[len(raw) - 1] == 10) {
    raw = raw[0:len(raw) - 1]
  }
  fsRemove(sh)
  fsRemove(out)
  let n = parseNonNegInt(raw)
  if (n < 1) {
    return 4
  }
  return n
}

// Each wave's own bound: the round count `xargs -P` actually needs for THIS
// corpus (`ceil(caseCount / n)`), not the fully-serial assumption the old
// combined bound made — see the header comment (#3586) for why that
// assumption was silently never honoured anyway. `outerSlackMs` covers
// `find`/`xargs`/startup overhead the round math does not model.
fn waveTimeoutMs(caseCount: int, cfg: Cfg, n: int): int {
  let rounds = (caseCount + n - 1) / n
  return rounds * cfg.timeoutMs + outerSlackMs
}

fn runOneWave(scratch: string, pattern: string, label: string, timeoutMs: int): (string, int) {
  let sh = "${scratch}/driver-${label}.sh"
  if (!writeWhole(sh, waveDriverScript(scratch, pattern, "the ${label} wave bound")) ||
    fsChmod(sh, scriptMode) != 0) {
    fatal("cannot write ${label} driver script at ${sh}")
  }
  return sh, osRunBounded(sh, timeoutMs)
}

// Phase B: one bounded `osRunBounded` call PER WAVE, not one for the whole
// corpus (#3586). The two calls run strictly in sequence from this single-
// threaded `main`, so the compile wave's call has fully returned — its
// direct child reaped whether by a clean exit or by the timeout/signal
// path's own `waitpid` (`osForkExecWaitBounded`, runtime/root/*/os.bit) —
// before the run wave's `find` ever executes: the ETXTBSY barrier (header
// comment) holds exactly as it did with one call.
fn runDriver(cfg: Cfg, scratch: string, caseCount: int): DriverRun {
  let n = detectParallelism(scratch)
  let t = waveTimeoutMs(caseCount, cfg, n)
  let (compileSh, compileResult) = runOneWave(scratch, "*.compile.sh", "compile", t)
  let (runSh, runResult) = runOneWave(scratch, "*.run.sh", "run", t)
  return DriverRun{
    compileSh: compileSh,
    runSh: runSh,
    compileResult: compileResult,
    runResult: runResult,
  }
}

// A plain exit of 1 (some case script itself failed) is not a driver-level
// anomaly — `gradeAll` already named it — so only warn on the other `decode`
// outcomes: spawn failure, timeout, or a signal.
fn reportDriverWarnings(driver: DriverRun) {
  if (driver.compileResult != 0 && driver.compileResult != 1) {
    eprint("imports: WARNING ${decode(driver.compileResult, "compile driver")}\n")
  }
  if (driver.runResult != 0 && driver.runResult != 1) {
    eprint("imports: WARNING ${decode(driver.runResult, "run driver")}\n")
  }
}

fn main() {
  let cfg = loadConfig()

  let scratch = scratchRoot()
  if (!shSafe(scratch)) {
    fatal("scratch path '${scratch}' is not shell-safe")
  }

  // Discovery only composes path STRINGS, so the directory is created after it
  // — a config or corpus failure then leaves nothing behind to clean up, and
  // `fatal` needs no unwinding of its own.
  let cases = discover(cfg, scratch)
  print("imports: discovered ${len(cases)} cases under ${cfg.corpus}\n")
  // Printed so a `Killed: 9`/`TIMED OUT` in this gate's log can be read
  // against the actual budget without opening the source (#3665 acceptance).
  print("imports: per-case timeout budget ${cfg.timeoutMs / 1000}s, retry budget ${cfg.timeoutMs / 1000 * retryTimeoutMultiplier}s\n")

  if (fsMkdir(scratch) < 0) {
    fatal("cannot create scratch directory ${scratch}")
  }
  writeScripts(cfg, cases, scratch)

  let driver = runDriver(cfg, scratch, len(cases))

  // #3593: a case whose OWN watchdog fired (compile or run, SIGKILL/137) gets
  // one retry, alone, before grading. #3665: the retry regenerates that
  // case's scripts with a LARGER budget (`retryTimeoutMultiplier`,
  // `grading.bit`) rather than rerunning the same bytes under the same
  // deadline — see `grading.bit`'s header note on `retryTimedOut` for why a
  // fresh, bigger budget is the fix for a false TIMED OUT under this box's
  // fleet contention, and how a genuine hang still gets caught (it times out
  // again on the retry, just with more rope, worst case bounded at two
  // budgets instead of one).
  let retried = retryTimedOut(cfg, cases, scratch)
  if (retried != 0) {
    print("imports: ${retried} case(s) retried serialized after their own watchdog fired\n")
  }

  let report = gradeAll(cases)

  // `gradeAll` above already read every `.rc` file off disk, so a wave's own
  // overrun (see `runDriver`) only costs the cases still in flight, reported
  // by `decodeRc` as "no exit status" — never the whole corpus's attribution.
  reportDriverWarnings(driver)

  // Read rather than discarded: if either wave's `xargs` reported a failing
  // case (1) but every case graded clean, the `.rc` bookkeeping is broken —
  // worse than any single named failure, and must not report as 0 failed.
  // `retried != 0` is the one legitimate exception: a case the FIRST wave's
  // `xargs` correctly reported as failing (its watchdog fired, script exited
  // 137) can still grade clean here because the retry actually fixed it —
  // that is the retry working as designed, not a bookkeeping bug.
  if (retried == 0 && report.failed == 0 && (driver.compileResult == 1 || driver.runResult == 1)) {
    fatal("imports driver: xargs reported a failing case script, but no case graded as failed — the .rc bookkeeping is broken")
  }

  cleanup(scratch, cases, driver.compileSh, driver.runSh)

  print("imports: ${len(cases) - report.failed}/${len(cases)} OK\n")
  if (report.failed != 0) {
    eprint("imports: FAILED:${report.summary}\n")
    osExit(1)
  }
}
